[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Basic Pattern 1"]
[Black "Central Setup (e4+d4)"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "20"]

1. e4 {This setup explains the main objective of open (1.e4) openings, why they are more dynamic than 1.d4 openings, and why they rarely look alike.} Nc6 2. d4
Nb8 {I play Black's moves only because lichess does not allow null moves.} 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Nc3 Nb8 5. Bc4 Nc6 6. O-O Nb8 7. Be3 Nc6 8. Re1
Nb8 9. Qd2 Nc6 10. Rad1 Nb8 {Basic idea: White places the pieces centrally. This is the most attacking setup, and Black will not allow it. White therefore has to adapt depending on Black's response. That is why it never occurs in this pure form. The best squares for the bishops naturally also depend on Black's setup.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Basic Pattern 2"]
[Black "Queenside Setup (c4+d4)"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "A50"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "24"]

1. d4 {This setup lies at the heart of all closed (1.d4) positions. It explains why play here is strategic, safe, and geared towards the endgame.} Nf6 2. c4 Ng8 {I play Black's moves only because lichess does not allow null moves.} 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 (4. g3 {only changes the setup slightly; the basic idea remains the same. This is how the Catalan is played, for example.} Ng8
5. Bg2 Nf6 6. O-O Ng8 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. b3 Ng8 9. Bb2 Nf6 10. Qc2 Ng8 11. Rac1 Nf6
12. Rfd1 Ng8 {The strategy is the same as in the main line.}) 4... Ng8 5. Be2 Nf6 6. O-O
Ng8 7. b3 {It is also possible to play Bg5 before e3 if the bishop would be shut in on b2.} Nf6 8. Bb2 Ng8 9. Qc2 Nf6 10. Nbd2 ({or, depending on the position,} 10. Nc3 {depending on Black's setup.} Ng8 11. Rac1 Nf6 12. Rfd1) 10...
Ng8 11. Rfd1 Nf6 12. Rac1 Ng8 {White has secured the king well. The entire army is concentrated on the queenside, playing for small positional advantages and generally being ready to use them in an endgame if Black chooses the symmetrical setup (1.d4 d5).} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Basic Pattern 3"]
[Black "Kingside Setup (e4+f4)"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "21"]

1. e4 Nc6 2. Nc3 Nb8 {I play Black's moves only because lichess does not allow null moves.} 3. f4 Nc6 4. Nf3 Nb8 5. g3 {Sometimes White also plays Bb5 here and exchanges the bishop.} Nc6 6. Bg2 Nb8 7. O-O Nc6 8. d3
Nb8 9. Be3 Nc6 10. Qd2 Nb8 11. Rae1 {White commits fully to a kingside attack and leaves the queenside to Black. Although this looks extremely aggressive, it scores less well in practice: against strong players the kingside attack often fails, while the queenside weaknesses become noticeable in the endgame. One might say that White is willing to sacrifice the endgame for the attack.} *

[Event "Isolated Queen's Pawn, Hanging Pawns, ..."]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Queenside Setup"]
[Black "Origins of Pawn Structures"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "A30"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "22"]

{The purpose here is not to explain the strategic play associated with these pawn structures, but to show why they arise so frequently and almost inevitably after 1.d4.} 1. d4
d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Be7 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O b6 7. b3 (7. cxd5 {This 'forces' Black to recapture with the pawn. Later, Black might also be able to recapture with a piece instead.} exd5 8. b3 Bb7 9. Bb2 c5 {see the main line}
(9... Bd6 10. Nc3 a6 {another typical pawn structure arising from 1.d4} 11. Ne5 $5 Nbd7
12. f4 {and so on: a closed game})) 7... Bb7 8. Bb2 c5 {If both sides adopt the basic setup (regardless of move order), the position is symmetrical. If one player voluntarily breaks the symmetry, or is forced to do so by the opponent's clever move order, all the familiar motifs arise.} 9. Nbd2 Nbd7 10. cxd5 exd5 11. dxc5 bxc5 {hanging-pawns structure.} (11... Nxc5 {isolated queen's pawn position}) *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Queenside Setup"]
[Black "Tarrasch Example"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D41"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "18"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 exd5 {Tarrasch Defence} (
5... Nxd5 {Semi-Tarrasch. Black accepts no weak pawns, but concedes the centre to White.} 6. e4 (6. e3 Nc6 7. Bc4 cxd4 8. exd4 Be7 9. O-O O-O {another isolated-queen's-pawn example, this time chosen voluntarily by White. In return for the weak pawn, White has the more active pieces, while the bishop on c8 is passive.}) 6... Nxc3
7. bxc3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 Nd7 12. O-O b6 {[%csl Ga7,Gb6,Yc5,Yd5,Gd7,Ge6,Gf7][%cal Gc8b7] An amusing situation: White has the central setup, while Black has the queenside setup!}) 6. g3 Be7 7. Bg2 O-O 8.
O-O Nc6 9. dxc5 Bxc5 {Black accepts the isolated queen's pawn in return for more active pieces.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Queenside Setup"]
[Black "Queen's Gambit Accepted Example"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D27"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "14"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 (3. e4 $5 {More aggressive, but also harder to play. Non-professionals usually avoid this move because it aims for a central structure, whereas 1.d4 players normally prefer closed positions.}) 3... Nf6 (3... e5 $5 {[%cal Yd7d5,Ye7e5] Black can also try the central setup!}) 4. Bxc4 e6 5. Nf3 c5 6. O-O a6 7. Nc3 b5 {[%csl Ya6,Yb5,Gc5,Gd5,Ge6,Gf7][%cal Gc8b7] Once again we see Black's typical queenside setup.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Queenside Setup"]
[Black "Meran Example"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D48"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[PlyCount "18"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 c6 {Black plays c6, but the aim is still to place the pawn on c5 later—without acquiring pawn weaknesses in the meantime.} 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 $1 (6... Bd6 {[%cal Ge6e5] The central approach also exists.}) 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 a6 9. e4 {example of the central setup} (9. O-O c5 {Once again Black has achieved the queenside setup; it is even a transposition to the Queen's Gambit Accepted, for example:} 10. Qe2 cxd4 11.
exd4 $5 {isolated queen's pawn example}) 9... c5 $132 {Here the two styles clash. White attacks, while Black seeks the more harmonious position.}
*

