1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 {hanging pawns} 3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 d5 {The Petrosian System—believe it or not, the second-most common setup against the Queen's Indian.} (3... b6 {Black adopts the queenside system.} 4. Nc3 (4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1
d5 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Nc3 c5 10. dxc5 bxc5 {[%csl Ga7,Gb6,Gc5,Gd5,Ge6,Gf7]}) 4... Bb7 5. a3 {is called the Bogo-Indian} d5
$1 {Black, in turn, adopts the central or even the kingside setup!} 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Qc2 Be7 8. e4 Nxc3
9. bxc3 O-O 10. Bd3 c5 {Black has almost achieved the queenside setup.}) (3... Bb4+ {Black plays with an isolated queen's pawn, but more aggressively. This already determines the plans that follow.} 4. Bd2 Qe7 5. g3 Nc6 6. Nc3 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 Ne4 8. Rc1 O-O 9.
Bg2 d6 10. O-O Nxc3 11. Rxc3 e5 {White does not have to play e4 and may continue with the queenside system.})) 3... Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 (4... O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3
b6 (6... d5 7. Nf3 dxc4 8. Qxc4 b6) 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. e3 d6 9. f3 Nbd7 10. Bd3 h6
11. Bh4 c5 {Black can now choose whether to play in the centre or on the kingside.}) 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 c5 {[%csl Yd5,Ye5] central setup} *

