1. e4 e5 {White has the centre.} 2. Nf3 (
2. d4 {The Ruy Lopez, the main line. White prepares d4 in the long term.} exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6
{The Scotch, a fully valid opening.}) 2... Nc6 (2... d6 $6 3. d4 $1 {the Scotch Four Knights}) 3. Bb5 {[%cal Gc2c3,Gd3d4,Ge2g3] The aim is d4.} (3. d4 {[%cal Gd3d4] The aim is d4.}) (3. Nc3
Nf6 4. Bb5 (4. d4 {The aim is d4.}) 4... Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3
Bxc3 (6... d6 7. Ne2 {[%cal Yd7d5,Rd3d4] The modern approach. White postpones d4 until later, while Black considers when ...d5 can be played.}) 7. bxc3 d6 8. Re1
{White has never forgotten about d4.}) (3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 {prepares d4} (5. d3 {White has achieved d4. Black now fights against White's centre.})) 3... a6 4. Ba4 Nf6
5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 $1 {[%cal Gd2d4] Here I show how Black punishes White by simply enforcing ...d5 against sidelines.} O-O 9. h3
$1 {White plays a gambit. Black does not have to accept it.} (9. d4 Bg4) 9... Bb7 10. d4 {[%csl Yd5,Ye5]} *

