1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 (2. Nc3 {White plays on the kingside, Black on the queenside.} Nc6 3. g3 (3. Bc4 {almost the pure form of the central setup} e6 4. d3 Nf6
5. Nf3 d5 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4] This is why it is the principled move.}) 3... d6 4. d3
g6 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. f4 Nf6 (6... e6 7. Nf3 Nge7 8. O-O O-O) 7. Nf3 O-O 8. O-O Rb8
{Here I no longer want to focus on White, but on Black. The Najdorf, the most feared opening, aims for}) 2... d6 (2... Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6
4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 d6 6. Be2 e6 7. Be3 Be7 8. O-O {[%cal Gf8d8, Ga8c8,Rd6d5] the queenside setup!}) 3. d4 $1 {[%cal Ge7e5] Black is aiming for the kingside setup.} cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Ye6e5,Ye5d4] The bishop on b2 is very poorly placed here. Black, by contrast, dispenses with ...b6 altogether because Black is not using the queenside setup.} 7.
Be2 Be7 8. O-O b5 9. a3 Bb7 10. f3 Nbd7 11. Qd2 O-O 12. Rad1 Qc7 {central setup} *

